From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: meanaverage <18336095+meanaverage@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 15:56:24 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fix(web): bound the pictures the H.264 decoders read back

WebCodecsH264Decoder reads a frame's regions back from its picture as
soon as the picture comes out of a decoder: into a buffer for their
RGBA (up to 256 MiB a frame), allocated before the copy starts, while
it keeps the picture until the copy is done. A frame leaves the frames
counted against the decoders' limits when its picture comes out, and
the stall check watches only those, so nothing bounded the readbacks in
flight. A server whose pictures came out faster than they were read
back (each region of a frame read on its own, say) had them pile up
with their buffers, and each one read was then copied into the
WebAssembly memory that all of a page's sessions share before
ironrdp-egfx counted it.

The readbacks in flight now take at most 512 MiB for the RGBA of their
regions, counted before a readback allocates and until it hands the
pixels back: two of the largest frames ironrdp-egfx reads back
(256 MiB, what its compositor holds), or four whole 8K pictures
(7680x4320, 127 MiB each). Regions read back as their bounding box take
the box as well while it is read, at most twice what they hold. A
picture that would go past the limit fails the decoder as a decoder
error does: the session ends with the reason, and the application can
reconnect without H.264.

The test of a region larger than its picture now uses one the limit
lets through (8192x8192): one of 65535x65535 is refused by the limit
before it is checked against the picture.
